    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Listen! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> It's a School Day!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Let's Listen!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="4" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Follow very simple classroom instructions with visual cues.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow simple instructions with visual cues (e.g., 'sit down' with gestures). Can follow visual signals to do tasks (e.g., 'clean up' supported with a picture). Can engage in games by responding to simple questions (e.g., 'What is this?' 'School.').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 60%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, children!" and uses a "Morning Song" that includes actions like "stand up" and "sit down." The teacher claps hands and says, "Let's listen!" (pronouncing /l/ in 'listen') while cupping an ear, encouraging students to mimic the action and sound. This connects to the theme "Let's Listen!" and introduces the action verb `listen` with a visual cue.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models "listen" by cupping their ear and making an exaggerated "shhh" sound, then pointing to their ears. The teacher then models simple classroom `imperatives` like "Stand up," "Sit down," and "Jump" using Total Physical Response (TPR) and gestures. The teacher also models the pronunciation of /s/ in `school` and /l/ in `listen`, emphasizing syllable stress in `teacher` and `student` by clapping for each syllable as they say the words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with simple visual and action-based questions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says "Stand up!" (while standing up). CCQ: "Are we sitting?" (Teacher shakes head 'no'). Students respond with gesture or "No."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher cups ear and says "Listen!" CCQ: "Do we talk now?" (Teacher shakes head 'no'). Students respond with gesture or "No."</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher also holds up a picture of a school and asks, "What is this?" encouraging students to respond "School!" with a clear /s/ sound.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's a School Day!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b> An audio recording of simple classroom instructions paired with large flashcards or realia (e.g., actual `book`, `chair`, `desk`, `door`, `window`). The recording will use a calm, clear voice giving commands. Genre: Audio instructions for kindergarteners. Register: Informal classroom, polite. Format: Short, distinct commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b> Following instructions: Respond to commands, like 'stand up' and 'listen'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher plays the audio (e.g., "Point to the chair!"). Students listen and point to the corresponding flashcard or actual object in the classroom (`chair`, `desk`, `book`, `window`, `door`). The teacher then asks, "What is this?" for each item, eliciting "It is a chair," "It is a book."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Move:</b> The teacher plays simple action commands (e.g., "Stand up!", "Touch the door!"). Students listen and perform the action. The teacher uses gestures to support understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen for Sounds:</b> The teacher plays audio of a bell ringing, then a teacher saying "Good morning, students!" The teacher asks, "What did you hear first? A bell or the teacher?" Students point to a picture of a bell or a `teacher` to indicate their answer. This activity practices initial sound awareness, especially `b` for `book` if a `book` sound is included, and `t` for `teacher`.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Following very simple classroom instructions with visual cues using `imperatives` (e.g., 'Stand up,' 'Touch,' 'Open'), `Present simple` ('It is a...'), and `WH-questions` ('What is it?'). Vocabulary: `chair`, `desk`, `table`, `book`, `window`, `door`, `teacher`, `student`, `listen`, `stand up`, `jump`, `touch`, `open`. Pronunciation: /s/ in `school`, /l/ in `listen`. Phonemic awareness: Syllable stress in `teacher` and `student`, initial sound /b/ in `book`, /l/ in `listen`, /t/ in `touch`. Pragmatic: Responding to commands. Sociolinguistic: Using 'Please' for politeness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Listen and Do - Classroom Commands":</b> The teacher gives clear, slow `imperative` commands. "Please, `stand up`." "Please, `sit down`." "Please, `touch` your `desk`." "Please, `open` your `book`." Students perform the actions. The teacher ensures correct actions and models polite language by consistently using "Please." Teacher emphasizes the /t/ sound in `touch`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Simon Says... School Day!":</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game using the target `vocabulary` and `imperatives`. "Simon says, '`Listen`!'" "Simon says, '`Jump`!'" "Simon says, '`Touch` the `window`!'" "Simon says, '`Open` the `door`!'" The teacher ensures students only follow commands preceded by "Simon says." This reinforces listening for key phrases and encourages active listening. The teacher can ask, "What is it?" pointing to a `book` or `chair`, and guide students to say "It is a book."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Teacher-led): "Listen and Follow the Leader":</b> Students work in small groups of 3-4. The teacher names one student in each group as the "leader" for a short turn. The teacher gives a command to the "leader," e.g., "Leader, `stand up` and `touch` your `chair`." The other students in the group observe if the leader follows the instructions correctly. The teacher circulates, observing listening comprehension and providing immediate feedback. The teacher uses `nice` and `calm` when giving praise or gentle corrections. For instance, "That was `nice` listening!" or "Be `calm` and `listen` carefully."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's a School Day!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Task Format:</b> Collaborative group activity using props and simple actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Activity: "Our School Scene – Listen and Build":</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups. Each group receives a mini-classroom mat or a large paper with outlines of a `desk`, `chair`, `table`. They also have small cut-outs of a `book`, a `student`, and a `teacher`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives a sequence of detailed `imperative` instructions. For example: "Please, put the `chair` `near` the `desk`." "Put the `book` on `top` of the `desk`." "Move the `student` `away` from the `table`." "Place the `teacher` `near` the `door`." (Teacher uses prepositions `near`, `away`, `top`, `bottom` here, reinforcing vocabulary).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students `listen` carefully and arrange their cut-outs according to the instructions. The task requires them to process multiple pieces of auditory information and translate them into physical actions, simulating setting up a classroom for a school day.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After each set of instructions, the teacher asks, "What is `near` the `desk`?" and expects students to point and say "The `chair`." or "It is a `chair`."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher observes groups for their ability to follow the instructions and collaborate.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task: "Listen to the Story, Do the Actions":</b> The teacher tells a very short, simple story about a `school day` using the target `vocabulary` and `imperatives`. For example: "It's `morning`. The `teacher` says, 'Good `morning`, `students`!' The `teacher` says, '`Please stand up`!' The `teacher` says, '`Touch` your `book`!' The `teacher` says, '`Open` your `book`!'" Students `listen` and perform the actions as prompted by the story. The teacher uses a checklist to note which students successfully follow 3 out of 4 commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly performing actions based on `imperatives` (e.g., `stand up`, `touch`, `open`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responding to `WH-questions` (e.g., 'What is this?') by pointing or single-word answers (e.g., 'Book.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Showing attention and `listening` behavior during instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Displaying basic `politeness` by responding to "Please" in commands.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a simple drawing of a face. The teacher asks, "Can you `listen` and `stand up`?" Students draw a happy face if "yes" or a sad face if "no." "Can you `listen` and `touch` the `book`?" (Same happy/sad face response). This provides immediate visual feedback on their perceived listening ability.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite `school` action to `listen` and do today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What helps you `listen` better in class?" (e.g., "teacher's voice," "seeing actions")</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to engage students in listening? (e.g., TPR, "Simon Says," visual cues).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time to improve students' ability to follow instructions? (e.g., slower pace, fewer instructions at once, clearer audio, more repetition of specific sounds like /l/ and /s/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the pronunciation and phonemic awareness goals (/s/, /l/, `teacher`/`student` stress, initial /b/, /l/, /t/) sufficiently addressed?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to Next Lesson:</b> This lesson focused on listening to basic classroom instructions and identifying school objects. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will introduce a simple illustrated story or short text using the same `school day` `vocabulary` (`chair`, `desk`, `book`, `teacher`, `student`, etc.), allowing students to begin associating the spoken words with their written forms in context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>